{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-127820",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Using the  addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 5}[3g(t)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to 5}f(t)+3{lim}_{t\\to 5}g(t) \\\\  & =f(5)+3g(5) \\\\  & =f(5)+3{{⟨-1,1,-2⟩}_{⏟}}_{g(5)} \\\\  & =f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)]=⟨1,3,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $f(5):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5}[f(t)+3g(t)] & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5)+⟨-3,3,-6⟩ & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨1,3,1⟩-⟨-3,3,-6⟩ \\\\ f(5) & =⟨4,0,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
